source(S['law'],'Lauren Benton, Law and Colonial Cultures: Legal Regimes in World History, 1400–1900 (Cambridge University Press, first-publication statement 2002), chapter 1 excerpt pp. 1–10 and selected front matter.','https://assets.cambridge.org/97805218/04141/excerpt/9780521804141_excerpt.pdf','Official ten-page chapter excerpt read in full, printed pp. 1–10: multisided legal contests pp. 1–3; institutional world history pp. 3–7; legal-system boundaries and state-centered narratives pp. 8–10. Book remainder unread. Copyright leaf visually checked in https://assets.cambridge.org/97805218/04141/frontmatter/9780521804141_frontmatter.pdf (PDF p. 4): copyright 2002, First published 2002, reprints 2003/2005 and digital printing 2007; hardback ISBN 9780521804141 and paperback 9780521009263. Cambridge Core https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/law-and-colonial-cultures/5DE1BB3C38E1F258F57ACC6A437BACF2 lists 3 December 2001 for print formats and 18 December 2009 online. The chronology follows the examined edition’s explicit first-publication statement, retaining the earlier platform release date as a separate observation rather than declaring it impossible. Acknowledgments p. xi explicitly credit Philip Curtin’s world-history training; no additional lineage edge is inferred.'),
source(S['sovereignty'],'Lauren Benton, A Search for Sovereignty: Law and Geography in European Empires, 1400–1900 (Cambridge University Press, first-publication statement 2010), chapter 1 excerpt and preface.','https://assets.cambridge.org/97805218/81050/excerpt/9780521881050_excerpt.pdf','Read chapter 1 printed pp. 1–5 and 7–10; p. 6 not used for a new claim in this packet. Corridors/enclaves pp. 2–3, divisible authority pp. 3–5, geographic conventions pp. 7–9 and mapping discussion p. 10 support bounded paraphrases. Later chapters unread. Front matter https://assets.cambridge.org/97805218/81050/frontmatter/9780521881050_frontmatter.pdf: copyright leaf visually checked at PDF p. 3, First published 2010; hardback ISBN 9780521881050 and paperback 9780521707435. Preface pp. xi–xii read for the author’s account of changing the project from ocean geography toward law and sovereignty; acknowledgments are not an influence register. Core https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/search-for-sovereignty/1FCB3767F56B64879CF13618A139866A gives 30 November 2009 print release and 5 June 2014 online. Keep these distinct observations; the selected chronology uses the edition’s 2010 first-publication statement.'),
source(S['interview'],'Lauren Benton and Dan Edelstein, “Of Pirates, Empire, and Terror: An Interview with Lauren Benton and Dan Edelstein,” Humanity 2, no. 1 (Spring 2011): 75–84, interview by Humanity.','https://humanityjournal.org/issue2-1/of-pirates-empire-and-terror-an-interview-with-lauren-benton-and-dan-edelstein/','Read Benton’s answers on pirates/legal authorization and legal geography (printed pp. 75–76 and 78–79), and her final extended answer on anomalous legal zones and violence (pp. 82–83), using the publisher HTML and its PDF https://humanityjournal.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/2.1-Of-Pirates-Empire-and-Terror.pdf . PDF running footer explicitly says Spring 2011; the website’s 10 June 2014 post date is a later upload. Dan Edelstein’s answers and the editorial questions are distinguished from Benton’s. Her account of Agamben is a reported critique; Agamben’s books and the interview’s modern legal examples were not independently checked. This source supports historical-method claims, not present legal advice or a general verdict on postcolonial history. A direct local download of the PDF returned 403; the tool-rendered publisher PDF and full publisher HTML were readable.'),
