node={'id':'lauren_benton','label':'Lauren Benton','date_label':'Colonial Law and Cultural Difference (1999) · Law and Colonial Cultures (edition imprint 2002) · A Search for Sovereignty (edition imprint 2010) · Humanity interview (2011) · with Richard J. Ross, Legal Pluralism and Empires (2013)',
'layer':'historians','period':None,'hunt_core_paradigm':False,
'description':'Studies jurisdictional conflicts, colonial legal practices and the uneven reach of imperial sovereignty. Her early book introduction connects repeated local legal contests with broader patterns of world history. A Search for Sovereignty examines how delegated authority and mobile subjects helped produce corridors, enclaves and differentiated legal spaces. In a later interview she argues that legal authorization could enable imperial violence even where authority was fragmented. The selected readings expose her historical questions and methodological distinctions; they do not make one account of legal pluralism binding on every empire.',
'representative_figures_and_works':'“Colonial Law and Cultural Difference” (1999; metadata and abstract only); Law and Colonial Cultures (examined edition states first published 2002; chapter 1 pp. 1–10 read); A Search for Sovereignty (examined edition states first published 2010; selected introduction and preface read); with Dan Edelstein, “Of Pirates, Empire, and Terror” (Humanity interview, 2011; selected Benton answers read); with Richard J. Ross, eds., Legal Pluralism and Empires, 1500–1850 (2013; bibliographic metadata only).',
'source_ids':[S[x] for x in ['identity','article','law','sovereignty','interview','edited']],
'entry_kind':'person','entry_type':'Individual historian',
'scope_note':'Selected legal-historical writings and first-person explanations, with later scholarship dated explicitly. Neither a full career review nor whole-book reading is claimed. Read Law and Colonial Cultures chapter 1 pp. 1–10; A Search for Sovereignty pp. 1–5 and 7–10 plus preface xi–xii; selected Benton answers in the 2011 Humanity interview pp. 75–76, 78–79 and 82–83. Original case records, later book chapters, the 1999 article body and the 2013 collection remain unread. The two copyright leaves explicitly state first publication 2002 and 2010, while Core lists earlier print-release dates 2001 and 2009; source notes preserve both observations. Dan Edelstein’s claims are not attributed to Benton. The postcolonial-history connection is a contextual contribution on colonial legal categories, not adherence to every postcolonial theory. Stern’s 2011 book is a separate, unread comparative resource, not evidence of influence. No lifespan, continuous career interval or review-through year is assigned.',
'chronology':{'version':1,'label':'Selected writings and interviews · 1999–2013','selection_note':'Selected dated works and witnesses, not lifespan, career boundaries or a field origin. Imprint statements, platform release metadata and website uploads are distinguished.','events':person_events,'intervals':[],'continuation':{'status':'not_asserted','basis':'No continuous duration or ending is established by these selected publications.'},'interpretation':'Each event retains the cited evidence scope. Later scholarship does not extend the review boundary of the fields to which the person is connected.','prepared_on':DAY,'editorial_record':'data/production-batches/person-entries-1.167/acceptance.json'}}
edges=[
{'proposal_id':'dc167_lb_global','source':'lauren_benton','target':'global','type':'connection','relationship_kind':'contribution','directed':True,'relationship':'Uses repeated jurisdictional conflicts and legal routines to connect local colonial encounters with institutional patterns in world history (Law and Colonial Cultures, 2002 imprint).','evidence_note':'Own introduction pp. 3–7 read, especially the move from multiple legal authorities to institutional world history. A particular comparative research strategy is being added; neither a founding of global history nor uniform legal development across all regions is asserted. The argument is not inferred from the dissertation citation count.','source_ids':[S['law']],'classification_basis':'editorial_review','map_label':'Jurisdictional politics across empires','roster_row':'global'},
{'proposal_id':'dc167_lb_post','source':'lauren_benton','target':'post','type':'connection','relationship_kind':'contribution','directed':True,'relationship':'Makes conflicts over legal forums, cultural difference and intermediaries part of explaining colonial authority, with divisions among colonizers and among colonized populations (2002 imprint).','evidence_note':'Own introduction pp. 1–3 and 8–10 read. Jurisdictional disputes are multisided; state authority is treated as historically produced through contests over law rather than a finished apparatus imposed intact. This is a contextual contribution to colonial/postcolonial historical inquiry, not a claim that Benton endorses a shared postcolonial doctrine or that legal strategies guaranteed emancipation.','source_ids':[S['law']],'classification_basis':'editorial_review','map_label':'Colonial difference and legal forums','roster_row':'post'},
{'proposal_id':'dc167_lb_spatial','source':'lauren_benton','target':'spatialhistory','type':'connection','relationship_kind':'contribution','directed':True,'relationship':'Reconstructs imperial space through legal corridors, enclaves and delegated authority rather than assuming even territorial sovereignty (A Search for Sovereignty, 2010 imprint; interview 2011).','evidence_note':'Introduction pp. 2–5 and 7–9 and preface xi–xii read; Benton’s interview answers pp. 75–76 and 82–83 explain the relation between legal practices, spatial patterns and authorized violence. Uneven authority is her historical argument, not proof that every empire lacked territorial ambitions. The nineteenth-century changes and the limits of extending the analogy to present situations remain explicit in her final answer.','source_ids':[S['sovereignty'],S['interview']],'classification_basis':'editorial_review','map_label':'Corridors, enclaves and uneven sovereignty','roster_row':'spatialhistory'}]
