strands=[
{'node':'global','strand':{'id':'dc167_imperial_legal_ordering','title':'Legal ordering across empires','focus':'Benton uses jurisdictional conflicts to ask how local cultural practices formed repeated institutional patterns across empires. This strategy places legal ordering alongside other ways of relating local encounters to global structures. Her 2002 introduction is read; the 2013 volume with Richard J. Ross and Stern’s separate 2011 corporate-sovereignty book are bibliographic resources, not evidence of a single school or transmission.','person_ids':['lauren_benton'],'works':'Benton, Law and Colonial Cultures (2002 imprint); Benton and Ross, eds., Legal Pluralism and Empires (2013, body unread). Further comparative resource: Philip J. Stern, The Company-State (2011, publisher metadata only).','source_ids':[S['law'],S['edited'],S['stern']],'basis':'editorial_distinction'}},
{'node':'post','strand':{'id':'dc167_jurisdiction_cultural_difference','title':'Cultural difference, legal forums and colonial authority','focus':'Benton treats legal classifications and forum disputes as elements in making colonial authority. The contests cut across divisions within colonizing and colonized populations. Her selected introduction asks how legal intermediaries and rival authorities altered the terms of rule; it does not treat either side as a coherent legal bloc or turn legal access into a guarantee of justice.','person_ids':['lauren_benton'],'works':'Law and Colonial Cultures (2002 imprint), chapter 1 pp. 1–10; “Colonial Law and Cultural Difference” (1999, abstract only).','source_ids':[S['law'],S['article']],'basis':'editorial_distinction'}},
{'node':'spatialhistory','strand':{'id':'dc167_uneven_imperial_sovereignty','title':'Uneven sovereignty: legal corridors and enclaves','focus':'Benton follows the relation between geographic descriptions, mobile subjects and delegated jurisdiction. Imperial space appears as differentiated corridors and enclaves; her account asks how these arrangements changed rather than treating all places as equally controlled or every legal anomaly as a temporary failure. The book’s full cases and later chapters remain unread here.','person_ids':['lauren_benton'],'works':'A Search for Sovereignty (2010 imprint), introduction pp. 1–5 and 7–10; Humanity interview (2011), selected Benton answers.','source_ids':[S['sovereignty'],S['interview']],'basis':'editorial_distinction'}}]
